From 46ae5dd0d937b870a9bc4d66e616d35b3ce60cfc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mptfire <263163432+mptfire@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 22:00:35 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 14/22] bash: QA follow-ups: the writer outlives the cap, the
 timeout ignores escapees

Second review pass, two probes reproduced and fixed:

Storage-capping the drains closed the read end at 2 MB, and the
writer on the far side died of SIGPIPE mid-run: a 3 MB writer's later
side effects silently never happened. The drain now keeps reading
past the cap and discarding (memory stays bounded, the pipe stays
open), so the command runs to completion and its marker lands.

A grandchild that setid()ed away escapes the process group, so the
timeout kill misses it and it holds the pipes open; the old joins
waited out the child's whole lifetime (probe: timeout 1 returned at
8s). The drains write into shared buffers and signal a channel:
collect gives the pipes a grace, kills the group, gives one last
grace, then abandons whatever is still open with the partial output
in hand, so no code path can block on a straggler. The timeout error
now carries that partial output, which is what the model needs to
course-correct.

Also hermetic: the allowlist test sets SSH_AUTH_SOCK itself (runners
have no agent), and the LC_ prefix travels as a family, not as two
hand-picked names.
